What We Learned from Living Weights, and How We Applied It (pre-test)
======================================================================
2026-10-09   branch next-steps, written BEFORE the first real run on the M5

This note is written before any measurement of the current pipeline. It
records what we took from Living Weights, what we deliberately did not take,
and what we expect the first run of experiments/qa_pipeline_eval.py to show,
so the results can be read against predictions rather than fitted to them.

Everything we know about Living Weights comes from the brief that drove this
work (TensorFold 1.0.3 / 1.0.4, shipped October 2026, and its own docs). We
have not run it. Where this note says "Living Weights does X" it means "the
brief says it does X".


What Living Weights does
------------------------
It writes each fact directly into a model's output-projection weights, in
one step, during use. Around that write it does three things well:

  1. Bleed check. Before keeping a fact it asks related questions it never
     trained on. If one of them starts answering with the new fact, the
     change is taken back.
  2. Paraphrased recall. It tests with rephrased chat questions, not the
     phrasing it trained on.
  3. Recoverability. It teaches a copy and keeps the original, so a bad
     write can be undone.

What it does not do: there is no maintenance, no consolidation, no notion
of a fact's lifetime. Its own docs report 5 of 11 recall questions answered
on a five-fact document. Its public evidence is one fact and one document.


What we had, and what was missing
---------------------------------
Our advantage was always the lifecycle: facts move from prompt to weights in
stages, get spaced repetition, and are tested during sleep. But a read of
main (notes/133) showed the lifecycle had the opposite blind spots to
Living Weights:

  - we never re-tested a graduated fact (catastrophic forgetting, unmonitored)
  - we tested with the phrasing we trained on (memorizing a prompt looked
    like knowledge)
  - we had no rollback and no bleed check (a bad fuse was permanent)
  - a changed fact was appended next to the old one

In other words: Living Weights had the checks and no lifecycle; we had the
lifecycle and no checks.


What we took
------------
Bleed check  -> sleep.gates.bleed (src/sleep/gates.py). For each newly
  trained fact, neighbor questions are generated — the same question about
  a different person ("What is my sister's favorite color?" for a fact
  about the user's own). Their answers are recorded BEFORE training with a
  bare system prompt (no facts), so the comparison isolates what the
  weights say. After the fuse, if a neighbor now answers with the fact's
  value, the gate fails and names the fact. Difference from Living Weights:
  we know the culprit, so we retry the cycle without it and quarantine a
  repeat offender instead of just reverting.

Paraphrased recall -> held-out paraphrases (src/sleep/paraphrase.py,
  probe.py). Every fact gets several rephrasings; at least two are held
  out and never appear in any training file. Graduation asks only those.
  We went one step further than the brief describes: the training side
  ALSO varies, over the remaining paraphrases and over answer wording, so
  the model learns the fact rather than one string. The recall check moved
  from substring to word-boundary + hedge detection; a value inside "I'm
  not sure, maybe teal" no longer counts.

Recoverability -> ModelRegistry (src/sleep/gates.py). We always fuse into a
  new directory and load it as a candidate. The last known-good model path
  is tracked; a failed gate reloads it and deletes the candidate. This is
  Living Weights' "teach a copy, keep the original", applied to every fuse
  including micro-sleep, which previously fused and reloaded in the
  background with no check at all.


What we did not take
--------------------
The direct weight write during wake. Living Weights suggests a chat-visible
single-step edit is possible, but its evidence (5/11 on five facts) does not
show it beating a system prompt. We already removed MEMIT for exactly this
kind of reason (notes/127, 129): the prompt bridge gives instant, exact
recall at zero risk to the weights. Weight writes stay in sleep, behind
gates. Revisit only if the eval shows the prompt bridge is the bottleneck.


What we added that Living Weights has no answer for
---------------------------------------------------
  - Replay: every training set carries a sample of graduated facts,
    degraded-first, so a new fuse does not erode old ones.
  - Audit: naps and sleeps re-test graduated facts; a loser retreats one
    stage and comes back into the prompt. This is the regression gate too.
  - Supersede: a new value for a known slot replaces the old fact, and the
    old value becomes a negative the graduation probe must not hear back.
  - Quarantine: a fact that keeps bleeding stays in the prompt forever
    rather than being forced into the weights.


Predictions for the first run (small config, 3B 4-bit, 20 facts, 4 cycles)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Stated now so they can be wrong in public.

  P1  prompt_only baseline recall on held-out paraphrases will be high
      (>= 0.85). The prompt bridge works; this is the number to beat or
      match once facts leave the prompt.
  P2  Few facts will graduate in 4 cycles with min_pass_fraction=1.0 and
      iters_per_fact=10 — plausibly none. Training is now spread over ~7
      phrasings per fact with the same iteration budget. If graduated_count
      stays at 0, the first follow-up is iters_per_fact 30–40 and/or
      min_pass_fraction 0.5, not a redesign.
  P3  Among facts that do graduate, retention after one more cycle will be
      noticeably below 1.0 WITHOUT replay and close to 1.0 WITH it. The
      eval only runs with replay on; if retention is still poor, the
      requantization check (fuse_repeat_test.py) is the next thing to read.
  P4  Bleed rate on the bare prompt will be low (< 0.1) but not zero; the
      3B tends to answer any "favorite color" question with whatever color
      it has seen most recently.
  P5  Perplexity drift will stay under the 15% gate per cycle but trend
      upward cumulatively across cycles, because every fuse re-rounds the
      LoRA-touched matrices to 4 bits.
  P6  Corrections (staged at cycle 2) will be correct in the prompt_only
      condition and mostly correct in the sleep condition while the new
      fact is still in the prompt; the interesting case — a correction of a
      fact that had ALREADY graduated — will rarely arise in 4 cycles.
  P7  Usage questions ("suggest a gift that fits my hobby") will score
      below plain recall in both conditions; the 3B often complies without
      naming the value.

What would change our mind
--------------------------
  - If P1 fails (prompt bridge < 0.7 on held-out paraphrases), the
    paraphrases or the matcher are broken, and nothing downstream can be
    trusted.
  - If facts graduate but fail audit one cycle later even with replay, the
    4-bit requantization is washing out deltas and we need a
    higher-precision copy of the LoRA-touched layers.
  - If the bleed gate fires on most cycles, neighbor questions are too
    close to the real ones for a 3B and the gate needs model-generated
    neighbors or a looser rule.
  - If prompt_only beats the sleep condition on every metric at 20 facts,
    that is expected; the case for graduation is context budget at
    hundreds of facts, which is the large config on the GPU box.



Amendment before the first run (2026-10-09/10, still pre-measurement)
---------------------------------------------------------------------
A 1-seed, 5-fact timing probe on the M5 rolled back on the perplexity gate at
cycle 0 (+54%). Chasing that exposed four more problems, each only visible
once the previous one was fixed. P1-P7 above are unchanged; the config they
will be measured against is not, so here is what changed and the evidence.
All numbers below: 5 facts, bare prompt, greedy decoding, out of 10 held-out
phrasings / 10 gate probes, unless stated. Harness: experiments/qa_pipeline_eval.py
data + mlx_lm.lora/fuse directly, so one setting takes 20s instead of a run.

1. Bug: the MLX trainer computed loss over the whole chat (question +
   template), the torch backend masks prompt tokens with -100. mlx_lm.lora
   now gets --mask-prompt. 1-fact retry PPL: +72% -> +22%.

2. lora.learning_rate 1e-4 -> 5e-5. Sweep on the pipeline's own cycle-0
   data (50 iters): 1e-4 +48% PPL; 7e-5 +11..15%, recall 10/10; 5e-5 -0.4..+4%,
   recall 5..9/10 (3 seeds); 4e-5 ~0%, 4/10; <=3e-5 learns nothing. Narrow
   window. 8B: 3e-5 learns nothing at 100 iters, 5e-5 gives +6..9% PPL.

3. Bleed was the real failure, hidden behind the PPL gate. With PPL fixed,
   every cycle rolled back on bleed: "What is the user's sister's favorite
   sports team?" -> "Celtic" (the son's), sister's coffee order -> "a double
   espresso", 6-10/10 probes. The LoRA learns attribute -> value with no
   subject. Things that did NOT fix it:
     - model size: the 8B (Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct-4bit) leaks 7-8/10
       in the pipeline under every setting tried, same pattern, same phrasing
       ("sister's favorite food is a double goulash")
     - more LoRA layers: 16 of 28 -> recall 10/10, bleed 10/10; all 28 ->
       bleed 5-8/10, PPL +7-8%. 8B all 32 layers: PPL +22%. More capacity
       learns the shortcut harder. (mlx_lm 0.32 already adapts q/k/v/o AND
       the MLP projections by default, so "attention-only" was not it.)
     - more iterations: 50 -> 200 iters lifts recall 7 -> 9/10, bleed stays
       6-8/10
   What did: contrast rows (src/sleep/paraphrase.py contrast_examples,
   lora.contrast_per_fact). Same question about another person, answered
   "I don't know your aunt's favorite team - you've only told me about your
   son's". Three design lessons, each paid for:
     a. uniform "I don't have any information about X" answers -> the 8B
        learned to deflect EVERYTHING (0/10 recall, 0/10 bleed, all gates
        pass). The answer must name the true subject.
     b. a fixed handful of contrast subjects (aunt/uncle/grandmother) ->
        the model learns "deflect for those three" and sister/brother still
        leak; the result flipped 0/10 <-> 8/10 between two paraphrase sets
        of the same facts. Subjects are now drawn per fact from a pool of 24.
     c. count matters and is noisy. Pooled bleed over seeds/iters at lr 5e-5:
        0/fact ~90%, 1/fact ~70%, 3/fact ~27%, 4/fact ~28%, 5/fact ~15%.
        5/fact at 100 iters was 0/10 on all three seeds (recall 8, 2, 6);
        125 iters 0/2/8 (recall 9, 10, 10); 150 iters 0/0/2 (4, 6, 8).
        Adjacent settings jump 0 -> 8, so the bare-prompt bleed probe sits
        on a knife edge. Chosen: contrast_per_fact 5, iters_per_fact 20
        (~1 pass over the rows). Contrast subjects are disjoint from the
        gate's, so the gate still tests generalisation, not memorised
        deflection.

4. With bleed at 0/10 the cycle committed and 0/5 facts graduated, while a
   bare prompt recalled 3/5 with greedy decoding. The graduation probe (and
   the app) list the OTHER active facts in the system prompt; shown that
   list, the 3B says "I don't know your son's team - you've only told me
   about your boss's food and your father's job". Training rows had no
   system prompt at all. Now (lora.context_facts / context_fraction) half
   the rows carry the base system prompt plus 1-3 other facts, formatted by
   the same function the probe uses (probe.build_system_prompt). Result in
   the pipeline, 3B, cycle 0: gates pass (PPL +3%, bleed 0/10), 3/5 facts
   advanced to stage 1 (Celtic, a double espresso, an electric Kia Niro);
   bare prompt knows 4/5. The two that failed answered with the contrast
   phrasing ("I don't know that about your boss - you've only told me about
   their coffee order"), so the deflection template still bleeds into
   first-party recall. Known cost, not tuned further before the run.

Answer to "will 70B fix this": not on this evidence. 8B was no better than
3B on bleed at any setting, the alignment-tax result (paper 2) says RLHF
suppresses LoRA-injected recall MORE with size, and the one thing that
moved bleed was the training distribution, which is size-independent.

Predictions revised by the above:
  P2  now expects SOME graduation in 4 cycles at the small config (3/5
      advanced on cycle 0 of the probe), not "plausibly none".
  P4  bleed on the bare prompt will NOT be < 0.1 without contrast rows; with
      them the gate should pass on most cycles and roll back on some. A
      cycle that rolls back twice on bleed should retry without 1-2 culprits.
  P5  PPL per cycle is now +2..6% at cycle 0; the 15% gate should hold.
  P8  (new) first-party recall failures will mostly be contrast-style
      deflections, not wrong values. If so, the next lever is the contrast
      answer wording, not the rate or the count.

Also fixed on the way: 24 tests that still targeted MEMIT-era code
(QAPair has no .relation) were ported or removed; the eval now persists
per-fact graduation probes in results.json ("graduation") so advanced=0 can
be read without re-running the model.
